--TEST--
All three response body members write to the same output `echo` does, in call order, so what a body member adds over an `echo` is the declaration and never a second buffer -- `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink` and `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink` binds a request's `echo` to the response body, so there is one
// place a body can be. § 4's members add the `Content-Type` to that. Asked of
// all three at once and by ordering, which is the shape that fails if any one
// of them grew a buffer of its own: a member accumulating separately would
// still print plausibly on a line by itself.
echo "0 ";
Core\Response::text("1 ");
Core\Response::json(2);
echo " ";
Core\Response::bytes("3" as bytes, "text/plain");
echo " 4\n";

// The same three under a capture, which is § 5's redirection of that one
// output: it takes all of them or none, and taking only `echo` would be the
// second buffer this design does not have.
var $captured = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    echo "a";
    Core\Response::text("b");
    Core\Response::json("c");
    Core\Response::bytes("d" as bytes, "text/plain");
});
echo $captured, "\n";
--EXPECT--
0 1 2 3 4
ab"c"d
